steps.body:
Checking every pair of a slice takes n * n steps. On **sorted** data, two indexes that start at both ends find a pair with a given sum in one pass. Compare the pair's sum with the target:

- Too small: `nums[i]` plus the biggest value left is still short, so `nums[i]` can't be in any answer. Move `i` right.
- Too big: `nums[j]` plus the smallest value left is still over, so move `j` left.
- Equal: you found the pair.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	nums := []int{1, 3, 4, 6, 9}
	target := 10
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
i, j := 0, len(nums)-1
for i < j {
	sum := nums[i] + nums[j]
	if sum == target {
		fmt.Println("found", nums[i], nums[j]) // found 1 9
		break
	} else if sum < target {
		i++
	} else {
		j--
	}
}
```

Each step rules out one value for good, so the loop runs at most n times. The condition `i < j` keeps the two indexes on different elements, and when they meet there is no pair. The data must be sorted from low to high, so sort it first with `sort.Ints` when it isn't.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	var n int
	fmt.Scan(&n)
	prices := make([]int, n)
	for i := range prices {
		fmt.Scan(&prices[i])
	}
	var q int
	fmt.Scan(&q)
	for ; q > 0; q-- {
		var target int
		fmt.Scan(&target)
		i, j := 0, n-1
		found := false
		for i < j {
			sum := prices[i] + prices[j]
			if sum == target {
				found = true
				break
			} else if sum < target {
				i++ // too small: drop the cheapest
			} else {
				j-- // too big: drop the most expensive
			}
		}
		if found {
			fmt.Printf("target %d: positions %d and %d\n", target, i+1, j+1)
		} else {
			fmt.Printf("target %d: none\n", target)
		}
	}
}

