steps.body:
Some windows have no fixed size: "the longest run with no repeated letter", "the longest run that stays within budget". Grow the window one step at a time with `right`, and whenever it breaks the rule, shrink it from the `left` until it's valid again. Then record it.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	s := "abcabcbb"
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(best)
}
```

```go
count := map[byte]int{} // letters inside the window
left, best := 0, 0
for right := 0; right < len(s); right++ {
	count[s[right]]++
	for count[s[right]] > 1 { // s[right] repeats: shrink until it's gone
		count[s[left]]--
		left++
	}
	if right-left+1 > best {
		best = right - left + 1
	}
}
```

The window is `s[left : right+1]`, so its length is `right-left+1`. Shrink with `for`, not `if`: one new letter can force several steps, such as when `b` returns after `abc`.

`right` visits every index once and `left` only moves forward, so the loop inside the loop still adds up to O(n) steps. That's the difference from checking every start and end, which is O(n * n).

example.code:
package main

import (
	"bufio"
	"fmt"
	"os"
)

func main() {
	scanner := bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin)
	for scanner.Scan() {
		s := scanner.Text()
		count := map[byte]int{}
		left, best, bestStart := 0, 0, 0
		for right := 0; right < len(s); right++ {
			count[s[right]]++
			for count[s[right]] > 1 {
				count[s[left]]--
				left++
			}
			if right-left+1 > best {
				best, bestStart = right-left+1, left
			}
		}
		fmt.Printf("%s: %s (%d)\n", s, s[bestStart:bestStart+best], best)
	}
}

